# Untitled

## Vacuum-Free and Solvent-Free Deposition of Electrodes for Roll-to-Roll Fabricated Perovskite Solar Cells
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are attracting widespread attention due to their exceptional photovoltaic performance and their potential for large-scale production via low-cost, high-throughput roll-to-roll (R2R) methods. Full realization of this production approach requires replacement of the evaporated metal electrode commonly used in PSCs. Here, a novel vacuum-free R2R-compatible method is introduced to fabricate and deposit printed electrodes based on electrically conductive pastes, which avoids potential loss of PSC performance due to solvent migration from the pastes. Flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs with record power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of up to \(16.7\%\) are produced by vacuum-free deposition of all functional layers, apart from the transparent conductive electrode. This performance compares very favorably with that of control flexible PSCs comprising an evaporated gold electrode, which displays PCEs of up to \(17.4\%\). Furthermore, the PSCs comprising a printed electrode demonstrate outstanding operational and mechanical stability, with negligible loss of PCE after \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) of continuous 1-sun illumination and retention of more than \(90\%\) of their initial PCE after 3000 cyclic bends.
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskites have recently attracted widespread attention in the photovoltaic (PV) community due
to their desirable optoelectronic properties. Since the first perovskite solar cell (PSC) was reported by Miyasaka et al. in 2009 having a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \(3.8\%\), the PCE of PSCs has increased to more than \(25.7\%\).[1,2] Whilst these highly efficient devices are typically fabricated on rigid glass substrates coated with a transparent conductive electrode (TCE), PSCs can also be fabricated on flexible polymeric substrates such as TCE-coated polyethylene terephthalate (PET) or polyethylene naphthalate. Flexible PSCs are more suited to a broad range of potential applications such as consumer electronics, conformable/wearable electronic textiles, vehicles, and building-integrated PV products.[3,4] The prospect of higher power-to-weight ratio also makes flexible PSCs attractive for space applications.[5] Despite these advantages, upscaling the production of flexible PSCs using high-throughput roll-to-roll (R2R) fabrication techniques is an ongoing challenge.
