Printed electrodes produced using carbon-based pastes and inks have emerged recently as a viable vacuum-free alternative to evaporated metal electrodes, exhibiting high electrical conductivity and a suitable work function \((-5.0\ \mathrm{eV})\) for charge
transfer.[19–23] Carbon-based electrodes are less expensive than evaporated metal electrodes and sufficiently mechanically robust to withstand repeated bending stresses.[24,25] Carbon-based PSCs have also been reported to demonstrate superior stability and lifetime compared to metal electrodes.[22] Flexible, spin-coated PSCs incorporating carbon-based electrodes with a PCE of more than \(15\%\) have been reported.[24] Solvent-based metallic nanoparticle inks have also been proposed as a printable alternative to evaporated metals due to their exceptional conductivity.[26] However, depositing a solvent-based paste or ink directly as the final layer in a printed PSC stack can be detrimental to the performance of the PSC due to solvent leaching from the paste/ink into other printed layers, or due to the application of heat in the drying or curing processes. Consequently, the range of pastes and inks that can be used for the printed electrode is greatly restricted by the limited number of "perovskite friendly" solvents, which is one of the factors that has constrained the highest PCE reported for a flexible R2R-fabricated PSC with a printed electrode to only \(10\%\) .[27] Therefore, an opportunity exists for significant improvements in the efficiency of flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs comprising printed, vacuum-free electrodes.
In the present work, we developed a new method for the R2R fabrication of flexible printed PSCs under ambient laboratory conditions. We found that upon compression, printed electrodes can adhere strongly to the surface of organic semiconductor materials. We fabricate a printed silver (Ag)/carbon electrode on an easily detachable substrate before transferring
the electrode onto a printed PSC precursor stack via dry press deposition (DPD). This enables the electrode to be deposited onto the PSC precursor stack without the underlying printed layers being exposed to heat or solvents. Using this method, devices displaying PCEs of up to \(16.7\%\) were produced, marking the highest reported efficiency for a flexible R2R-printed PSC comprising a vacuum-free "back electrode." The flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs with a printed DPD electrode were also found to display outstanding mechanical stability with marginal loss of PV performance after 3000 cyclic bends. The approach described in this work provides a cost-effective, vacuum-free, and solvent-free alternative to the expensive evaporated deposition of metal electrodes, which is an important milestone towards the commercial viability of PSCs.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Printed Electrode Fabrication
The fabrication steps used for the printed electrode are illustrated in Figure 1. A highly conductive Ag paste was bar-coated onto the silicone-coated side of PET film "release layer." The coated Ag paste was then dried for \(2\,\mathrm{min}\) on a hot-plate at \(135\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to evaporate the solvents on the coated wet film. A uniform layer of a carbon-based paste was then similarly bar-coated onto the dry Ag layer, followed by the same thermal treatment. Various other up-scalable methods can also be used to apply
these two layers, such as slot-die coating or screen printing. The flexible Ag/carbon bilayer was then attached to the hole-transport layer (HTL) of a R2R-fabricated PSC precursor "stack" (PET/TCE/ETL/perovskite/HTL, ETL = electron-transport layer) via compression in a calendar press. This DPD step can also be achieved using other roll-lamination techniques, uniaxial pressing, or isostatic pressing. Following deposition of the Ag/carbon bilayer to complete the PSC, the release layer was then peeled away to expose the printed Ag film for electrical connection. The release layer can subsequently be reused for the fabrication of further printed Ag/carbon electrodes.
The flexible PSC precursor stacks were prepared separately to the Ag/carbon electrodes by sequential deposition of the respective functional layers using readily scalable R2R coating techniques, namely slot-die coating and reverse-gravure coating, as illustrated in Figure 2a. Whilst the perovskite layer
and the \(2,2',7',7'\)-tetrakis-(N,N-di-4-methoxyphenylamino)-9,9'-spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD) HTL were deposited via slot-die coating, the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) ETL was deposited using reverse-gravure coating as we were able to achieve better solution wetting and a more uniform film. The PSCs were fabricated entirely in an ambient laboratory environment (see the Supporting Information for further experimental detail) and have conventional \(n\)-\(i\)-\(p\) architecture; PET/TCE/\(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{F}_{0.05}\mathrm{Si}\mathrm{MA}_{0.14}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.83}\mathrm{Br}_{0.17})_{3}/\) Spiro-OMeTAD/carbon/Ag. The final device architecture is illustrated in Figure 2b.
The thermal treatment of the Ag-based and carbon-based coatings prior to contacting the R2R-fabricated PSC precursor stack is crucial for avoiding potential solvent diffusion into other functional layers of the device, which can lead to rapid device degradation. As such, the DPD method used in the present work can be considered as "solvent-free," enabling the use
of conductive pastes containing solvents that might otherwise have adverse effects on PSC performance. The benefit of using a separately prepared Ag/carbon electrode for PSC fabrication is demonstrated in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information, showing the markedly inferior PV performance obtained for the flexible PSCs when the electrode pastes were coated directly onto the HTL of the PSC precursor stack.
Clearly, good interface contact between the carbon film of the printed electrode and the HTL must be established for optimal PSC efficiency. In this work, good contact was achieved by passing the PSC precursor stack together with the printed Ag/carbon stack through a calendar press, as illustrated in Figure 2c. Photos of the electrode DPD process and calendar press are shown in Figure S2 in the Supporting Information. The applied pressure was adjusted by varying the distance between the two rollers of the calendar press, monitored using two electronic sensors having an accuracy of \(10\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) and thickness gauge sticks. The compression, \(P_{\mathrm{comp}}\), was calculated as a percentage by measuring the total thickness of the PSC precursor stack plus the printed electrode stack before lamination, \(h_{1}\), and the distance between the two rollers of the calendar press, \(h_{2}\), according to Equation (1).
\[
P_{\mathrm{comp}}=\frac{h_1-h_2}{h_1}\times100\%
\]
The performance of PSC devices was monitored as a function of \(P_{\mathrm{comp}}\) between \(12\%\) and \(60\%\). A compression of \(12\%\) was found to be the lowest value needed to achieve adequate adhesion between the printed carbon layer and the HTL of the PSC precursor stack. The box plot in Figure 2d shows the normalized PCE results as a function of increasing \(P_{\mathrm{comp}}\), with the \(17\%\) and \(20\%\) variants giving the best-performing devices. Light-beam induced current (LBIC) mapping was performed to evaluate the effect of compression on photocurrent generation. Figure 2e indicates an optimized \(P_{\mathrm{comp}}\) value of \(17{-}25\%\), displaying a significantly greater photocurrent area (yellow region). This is compared to the other \(P_{\mathrm{comp}}\) values which displayed regions of delamination \((12\%)\) or device damage \((40\%\) and \(60\%)\).
The bilayered electrode thickness was also optimized to facilitate efficient charge transfer and permit a high degree of flexibility. The Ag film is comprised of tightly packed Ag particles (morphology shown in Figure S3, Supporting Information), which aids the charge collection and lateral charge transport to the electrical contacts. This is a particularly critical design consideration for large area devices and modules to maximize the fill factor (FF) by reducing the series resistance of the cell. An optimized Ag film thickness of approximately \(20\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) exhibits high conductivity (around \(50\,\mathrm{m}\Omega\,\square^{-1}\)) and good mechanical flexibility to withstand repeated bending stresses. Furthermore, the macroporous carbon film (Figure S3, Supporting Information) is integral to establishing strong interface contact with the receiving PSC precursor stack. Without this carbon interlayer, the printed Ag electrodes were found to easily delaminate from the PSC precursor stack and hence were not suitable for the flexible PSCs defined herein. The carbon film thickness strongly influences both its flexibility and its conductivity, two critical parameters for flexible PSCs.
The sheet resistance for various carbon film thicknesses is provided in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information and demonstrates an inverse relationship between film thickness and conductivity. It was also found that carbon thicknesses below \(15\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) resulted in an exponential increase in sheet resistance. Conversely, we found that when the carbon films were too thick (around \(70\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\)), the film would often crack or snap upon bending, thus being unsuitable for this application. Therefore, the carbon film thickness was optimized to balance the two critical performance factors: conductivity, and flexibility. Carbon dry-film thicknesses (before transfer to the PSC precursor stack) were varied from 3 to \(70\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) and the effect on the PSC device performance is shown in Figure 2f. Poor interfacial contact was observed when the carbon layer was too thin \((\leq5\ \upmu\mathrm{m})\), resulting in reduced charge carrier collection and thus lower PSC device performance. For carbon film thicknesses of \(10{-}50\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\), there were marginal differences in the PSC device performance and all electrodes exhibited excellent electrode adhesion to the PSC precursor stack. For carbon films of approximately \(70\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) thickness, whilst some devices still exhibited high performance, the thick bilayered electrodes were significantly more fragile and prone to cracking and delamination during transfer to the PSC precursor stack. Balancing the factors of flexibility and conductivity, an optimized carbon film thickness of \(30{-}40\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) was subsequently adopted.
Single layer printed carbon electrodes without the printed Ag layer also displayed adequate adhesion to the HTL following DPD onto the PSC precursor stack, although the poor lateral conductivity of the carbon electrode alone resulted in lower PV performance. For the bilayer electrode approach used in the present work, the macroporous carbon layer plays a critical in forming an interlayer between the PSC precursor stack and the conductive printed Ag layer. We also investigated various organic semiconductor materials as indicated in Table S1 in the Supporting Information and believe the DPD technique can be used with a wide range of materials with alternative material combinations currently being investigated.
## 2.2. Performance of Optimized PSCs
The \(J{-}V\) curves in forward and reverse scan directions, along with the PV parameters for the champion flexible, fully printed, and R2R-fabricated PSC are presented in Figure 3a. A shadow mask was used to define an active cell area of \(0.08\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\). The champion device displayed a PCE of \(16.7\%\) \((V_{\mathrm{oc}} = 1.17\ \mathrm{V}, J_{\mathrm{sc}} = 20.9\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}, \mathrm{FF} = 68.1\%)\) in a reverse scan direction. To our knowledge, this result represents the highest recorded efficiency to date for a flexible, R2R-fabricated PSC produced by vacuum-free deposition of all functional layers, apart from the TCE (Table S2, Supporting Information). The forward direction \(J{-}V\) scan indicates minimal hysteresis, with a PCE of \(16.0\%\) \((V_{\mathrm{oc}} = 1.17\ \mathrm{V}, J_{\mathrm{sc}} = 21.5\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}, \mathrm{FF} = 63.7\%)\). Maximum power point (MPP) tracking of the champion PSC with printed DPD Ag/carbon electrode (Figure 3b) indicates a stabilized PCE of \(16.0\%\) for over \(80\ \mathrm{s}\) under continuous 1-sun illumination. Furthermore, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) and cumulative \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) (Figure 3c) indicate broad light absorption in the visible wavelength range and validate the \(J_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of approximately \(20\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2}\).
Flexible devices having an evaporated Au electrode were also fabricated from the same batch of PSC precursor stacks, as controls. A comparison between the \( J{-}V \) curves and PV parameters of the champion PSC with an evaporated Au-electrode and that with the printed DPD electrode is shown in Figure 3d. The best Au-electrode device displayed a PCE of \( 17.4\% \) \( \mathrm{V_{oc}}=1.15\ \mathrm{V} \), \( J_{\mathrm{sc}}=21.6\ \mathrm{mA\ cm}^{-2} \), and \( \mathrm{FF}=69.8\% \) in the reverse scan, only marginally higher than the champion PSC with the printed DPD electrode, and marking a record PCE for a flexible R2R-coated PSC with an evaporated Au electrode.
The reproducibility of the DPD fabrication method developed in this work, compared to the Au-electrode devices, is demonstrated in the box plot in Figure 3e. The PSCs with printed DPD electrodes exhibited outstanding reproducibility and were only out-performed by a couple of the evaporated Au electrode PSCs. This marginally lower performance is attributed, at least in part, to the lack of reflection of residual transmitted incident light from the printed Ag/carbon electrode back into the perovskite layer, compared to the highly reflective Au electrode.
## 2.3. Operational and Mechanical Stability
The operational stability of the flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs produced in this work was investigated under MPP conditions at 1-sun illumination in an ambient laboratory environment. The PSCs were encapsulated within flexible polymeric barrier materials to protect them from degradation due to ambient
The operational stability of the flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs produced in this work was investigated under MPP conditions at 1-sun illumination in an ambient laboratory environment. The PSCs were encapsulated within flexible polymeric barrier materials to protect them from degradation due to ambient moisture and oxygen.[28] The PSCs and barrier materials were all appropriately preconditioned prior to encapsulation to mitigate the risk of moisture and oxygen outgassing.[29] The encapsulation architecture is illustrated in Figure 4a. A \( 200\ \mathrm{nm} \) thick Ag pattern was evaporated onto the back-barrier film to enable electrical contact with the device. Electrically conductive adhesive tape (conductive adh.) was used to improve the electrical connection between the printed Ag/carbon electrode and the evaporated Ag pattern. A photo of the fully encapsulated PSC device is shown in the inset in Figure 4b. As shown in Figure 4b, PSCs exhibited negligible loss of PCE after \( 24\ \mathrm{h} \) of MPP tracking under continuous 1-sun intensity illumination in an ambient laboratory atmosphere \( (21\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\,40{-}60\%\ \mathrm{RH}) \). The slight variation seen in the PCE is due to a sporadic flickering of the illumination lamp throughout the test period.
Cyclic-bending fatigue testing was conducted to evaluate the mechanical robustness of the R2R-fabricated PSC devices and to compare the printed DPD electrode with control PSCs having an evaporated Au electrode. The bending test was performed on unencapsulated devices in an ambient laboratory environment using an in-house constructed bending apparatus (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The devices were subjected to convex and concave bending with a bending radius of \( 10\ \mathrm{mm} \). These bending regimes applied tensile (convex) or compressive (concave) stress to the electrode, and one cycle represents the sequence: flat \( \rightarrow \) bend \( \rightarrow \) flat. Images of a PSC with a printed DPD electrode in concave and convex bending are shown in Figure S4 in the Supporting Information. A reference
device was used to assess the stability of devices when stored in the ambient environment for the duration of the bending test, as indicated by the grey lines in Figure 4d. After 3000 bending cycles, there were no visible signs of damage or delamination of the printed electrodes, indicating good adhesion between the carbon and Spiro-OMeTAD layers. As seen in Figure 4d, the PSC device with printed DPD electrode exhibited outstanding mechanical robustness, retaining over \(90\%\) of its initial PCE after 3000 convex bends in the ambient laboratory environment. This is directly comparable to the Au-electrode PSC which retained about \(93\%\) of its initial PCE after the same number of convex bends.
Slightly more degradation was observed when the devices were subjected to concave bending. The PSCs with printed DPD or evaporated-Au electrodes exhibited almost identical performance after 3000 concave bending cycles, retaining just under \(80\%\) of their initial PCE. To further investigate the cause of this degradation, resistance of the printed DPD electrode was measured using a four-point probe (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Concave bending was found to be more damaging to the electrode and resulted in an increase in sheet resistance, particularly under extreme bending (5 mm radius). Whilst there is only a slight increase in sheet resistance between the \(10\ \mathrm{mm}\) radius and \(5\ \mathrm{mm}\) radius for convex bending, an increase of more than \(350\%\) is observed when the electrode is subjected to only 500 concave bending cycles with a \(5\ \mathrm{mm}\) radius. This increase in resistance with concave bending is assumed to contribute to the observed decrease in PCE seen
in the mechanical fatigue tests. Despite this, not only did the PSCs with printed DPD electrodes exhibit comparable mechanical robustness to the PSCs with evaporated Au electrodes, there were also no signs of delamination or film damage for the printed devices even under the harsh \(5\ \mathrm{mm}\) radius bending regime.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a vacuum-free and R2R-compatible printed electrode deposition method which mitigates the risk of PSC degradation that can arise from solvent leaching or thermal treatment processes used in other printed electrode processes. We have demonstrated flexible R2R-fabricated PSCs with PCEs up to \(16.7\%\) for fully printed devices including a printed Ag/carbon back electrode, and up to \(17.4\%\) for devices having an evaporated Au back electrode. The comparable PCE of the PSCs with an evaporated Au electrode and the PSCs with a printed Ag/carbon electrode demonstrates great promise for the electrode dry press deposition method developed here, and suggests even further improvements may be possible. The flexible PSCs with printed DPD electrodes also exhibit outstanding bending durability, indicating their suitability for a wide range of flexible electronic applications. Our further work in this area will involve further optimization of printed electrode transfer methods and electrode materials, along with upscaled demonstrations.
